; nospf.mail.test
;
; Has TXT records and none of them is SPF, which is the realistic shape of an unprotected domain
; rather than a domain with no TXT set at all. It is also the fixture that separates "the query
; returned nothing" from "the query returned records and none was the one we wanted", and those
; two are the same result to a checker that reads the first answer.
;
; Addresses come from the RFC 5737 documentation range and the domain is under .test, which
; RFC 2606 reserves, so nothing here can route anywhere real.

$ORIGIN nospf.mail.test.
$TTL 60

@       IN  SOA  ns1.nospf.mail.test. admin.nospf.mail.test. (
                 2026092401 ; serial
                 3600       ; refresh
                 900        ; retry
                 604800     ; expire
                 60 )       ; minimum

@       IN  NS   ns1.nospf.mail.test.
ns1     IN  A    127.0.0.1

@       IN  A    192.0.2.40
mail    IN  A    192.0.2.41
@       IN  MX   10 mail.nospf.mail.test.

; Three vendor verification tokens and no SPF. This is the ordering that breaks a first-answer
; checker on a real domain, reproduced deliberately.
@       IN  TXT  "google-site-verification=rX9Qz1vK3mQ8pLd2wYt7hN4bC6jF0sA1"
@       IN  TXT  "MS=ms48271639"
@       IN  TXT  "atlassian-domain-verification=9pQz2mKxRt4vB7nD"

; No DKIM selector and no DMARC either, so this domain is spoofable by anybody.
